Nice & Renice - The nice and renice commands are used to control process scheduling priority in Unix/Linux systems. 
They determine how much CPU time a process receives relative to other processes.
  - Key idea: A lower nice value means higher priority, while a higher nice value means lower priority.
  Typical nice values range from -20 (highest priority) to 0 (Default priority) to  19 (lowest priority).
  - Note: Decreasing the nice value (giving a process higher priority) usually requires root or appropriate privileges. 
  Regular users can typically only increase the nice value (lower a process's priority).

nice — Start a Process with a Specific Priority....Launches a new process with a specified scheduling priority.
Syntax: nice [OPTION] [COMMAND [ARG]...] 
nice -n NICE_VALUE command 
Flag              	            Description
-n VALUE	              Set the nice adjustment (or absolute value, depending on implementation)
--adjustment=VALUE    	Long form of -n
--help	                Display help information
--version	              Display version information

Note: On GNU/Linux, if nice  -n value is not specified, nice -n VALUE command adjusts the process's nice value by VALUE relative to the current value. 
Since most shells start with a nice value of 0, nice -n 10 typically results in a nice value of 10.
USECASE EXAMPLES:
*nice -n 10 python analysis.py # Start a program with lower priority, The Python script runs with a lower CPU priority, making it less likely to impact interactive tasks.
*nice -n 15 tar -czf backup.tar.gz Projects/ #Compress a large directory without slowing your computer, The compression runs in the background with reduced priority.

renice — Change the Priority of a Running Process...Unlike nice, renice modifies the priority of an already running process.
Syntax: renice priority [options] identifier #The identifier can be: a Process ID (PID), a user, a process group.
Flag	             Description
-n VALUE	    Specify the new nice value (on many systems, the first positional argument is more commonly used)
-p	          Target one or more process IDs  #renice 19 -p 4128 - reduces CPU process priority of this process
-u	          Target all processes owned by a user #sudo renice -7 -u alice #increase CPU scheduling priorities of all processes owned by alice.
-g	          Target a process group #renice 5 -g 1001 #Updates every process in process group 1001.
--help	      Show help
--version	    Show version
Note: increasing priorities requires, ps aux | grep -w "firefox", top, htop - to get PID, user or PG before running renice.

jobs, bg, fg, nohup, & (background execution)
These commands are part of job control in Bash. They let you start, pause, resume, and manage processes running in the foreground or background of your current shell.
Key Concepts
Foreground process: Occupies the terminal until it finishes (e.g., vim, nano, python script.py).
Background process: Runs without taking over the terminal, allowing you to continue entering commands.
Job: A process or pipeline managed by the current shell. Jobs are numbered (e.g., [1], [2]) and are different from process IDs (PIDs).

jobs — List Background and Suspended Jobs....Displays the jobs currently managed by the current shell session.
Syntax: jobs [options] [job_spec]
Symbols:
+ = current/default job (most recently foregrounded or backgrounded)
- = previous job (second most recent)
Flag	          Description
-l  	Show the job number and the process ID (PID)
-p	  Display only the PIDs of jobs
-r	  Show only running jobs
-s	  Show only stopped (suspended) jobs
-n	  Show only jobs whose status has changed since the last notification
